---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'Observability Overview', href: '/docs/observability' },
  { title: 'SIEM & Log Pipeline', href: '/docs/observability/siem', active: true },
  { title: 'Kibana Dashboards', href: '/docs/observability/kibana' },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'dashboard', text: 'Dashboard Management', level: 2 },
  { id: 'timeline', text: 'Timeline Visualization', level: 2 },
  { id: 'dataflow', text: 'Data Flow', level: 2 },
  { id: 'artifacts', text: 'Key Artifacts', level: 2 },
  { id: 'contracts', text: 'Pipeline Contracts', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="SIEM & Log Pipeline" description="How logs are processed and analyzed using SIEM tools" section="Observability" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    The SIEM path is intentionally one-directional. The edge writes structured events and ships them out over
    mTLS; the SIEM host indexes and visualises them. Nothing in the request path waits for Elasticsearch or
    Kibana to answer.
  </p>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <DocCard title="Offline first" icon="archive" tag="Idempotent" description="Dashboard objects are generated from Python into NDJSON, so a rebuild does not depend on a live Kibana." meta="build_dashboards.py" />
    <DocCard title="Live sync" icon="branch" tag="Saved Objects API" description="The same tooling can import into and export from a running Kibana instance to preserve manual edits." meta="export_dashboards.py" />
    <DocCard title="Visual evidence" icon="eye" tag="Timeline" description="Embed-mode screenshots capture a specific pattern's request timeline for reports and the thesis appendix." meta="render-complete wait" />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="dashboard">Dashboard Management</h2>
  <p>Honeypot dashboards are managed through a combination of offline generation and live Kibana interactions.</p>
  <h3>1. Dashboard Building and Export</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Offline Building:</strong> <code>siem/kibana/build_dashboards.py</code> generates dashboard objects and visualizations into a local NDJSON file. Idempotent, can be run offline.</li>
    <li><strong>Live Kibana Interaction:</strong> Same script can interact with a live Kibana instance via Saved Objects API (using KIBANA_URL, ELASTIC_USERNAME, ELASTIC_PASSWORD env vars).</li>
    <li><strong>Exporting Live Changes:</strong> <code>siem/kibana/export_dashboards.py</code> exports live dashboard objects from Kibana to local NDJSON. Used after manually editing panels in live Kibana.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>2. Timeline Visualization</h3>
  <p>A key component is the pattern report time-series panel, which visualizes requests captured by the reverse proxy. The client-side component (<code>dashboard/ui/kibana_timeline_capture.py</code>) handles the capture process: automates login, imports saved objects via Kibana API, loads the timeline dashboard in embed mode, waits for render-complete signal, captures screenshot.</p>

  <h2 id="dataflow">Data Flow Architecture</h2>
  <pre><code>Client Request → Nginx access_by_lua_block → pattern_analyzer.analyze_request()
  → security events → Nginx error log → Vector → Elasticsearch
  → router.decide_route() → routing decision → honeypot pool or production
  → session_handler.update_session() → Redis session_store
  → Suricata eve.json → init_worker.lua → threat_intel shared dict
  → abuseipdb_client.report_ip_async() → AbuseIPDB</code></pre>

  <h2 id="artifacts">Key Artifacts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>siem/kibana/build_dashboards.py</code> — Script for generating and managing dashboard configuration.</li>
    <li><code>siem/kibana/export_dashboards.py</code> — Script for synchronizing live Kibana state to disk.</li>
    <li><code>dashboard/core/kibana_timeline.py</code> — Defines structure and payload for request timeline visualization.</li>
    <li><code>dashboard/ui/kibana_timeline_capture.py</code> — Handles client-side workflow for live dashboard capture.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="contracts">Pipeline Contracts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Events are JSON lines.</strong> <code>log_security_event()</code> emits a single JSON object per line, prefixed so Vector can filter without parsing unrelated Nginx errors.</li>
    <li><strong>Arrays are arrays.</strong> Empty Lua tables are tagged with <code>cjson.empty_array_mt</code>, so they encode as <code>[]</code> instead of <code>{}</code> and do not trigger Elasticsearch mapping conflicts.</li>
    <li><strong>Retries never block the edge.</strong> Vector buffers locally; a SIEM outage shows up as lag, not as failed requests.</li>
    <li><strong>Dashboards are code.</strong> Saved objects are generated from Python and committed as NDJSON, which makes reviews and rollbacks possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Exports preserve manual work.</strong> If a panel was edited live, export it back to NDJSON instead of rebuilding over it.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
